## STEP 3: Schema Verification Report

| # | Question | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | readConversations() implementation | Reads file → `JSON.parse()` → if array, normalises to object keyed by `conversation_id` → returns object. Graceful empty `{}` on error. |
| 2 | writeConversations() implementation | `fs.writeFileSync()` serialises the full object as pretty-printed JSON. Full overwrite, not append. |
| 3 | conversations.json current format | Object — keyed by `conversation_id` strings (e.g. `"local-chat": {...}`) |
| 4 | Frontend expectation | API returns `Object.values(conversations)` as an array. Frontend consumes an array of conversation objects. |
| 5 | Safest append format for system conversations | Each conversation is an object with `conversation_id`, `title`, `category`, `messages[]`, `created_at`, `updated_at`. Messages are appended as `{ sender, text, metadata, timestamp }`. |
| 6 | Direct worker-side write vs API endpoint | API endpoint is safer — `writeConversations()` does a full object overwrite, not an append. A race condition between the Web Console and the worker writing directly at the same time would corrupt the file. The Web Console's `POST /api/conversations/:id/messages` endpoint handles atomic message appends correctly. |

